        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Bill C-3 changes to citizenship by descent took effect</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">In this guide</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-blue-700">
        <li><a href="#what" class="hover:underline">What a citizenship certificate is (and is not)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#who" class="hover:underline">Who needs proof of Canadian citizenship</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bill-c3" class="hover:underline">Bill C-3 and citizenship by descent</a></li>
        <li><a href="#apply" class="hover:underline">How to apply: online or form CIT 0001</a></li>
        <li><a href="#documents" class="hover:underline">Documents, photos and fee</a></li>
        <li><a href="#processing" class="hover:underline">Processing time and checking your status</a></li>
        <li><a href="#e-certificate" class="hover:underline">E-certificate or paper certificate</a></li>
        <li><a href="#replace" class="hover:underline">Replacing or updating a certificate</a></li>
        <li><a href="#passport" class="hover:underline">Using your certificate for a passport</a></li>
        <li><a href="#grant" class="hover:underline">Not a citizen yet? The citizenship grant for PRs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        The Canadian citizenship certificate is one of the most requested documents IRCC issues, and one of the slowest. Demand jumped after Bill C-3 widened citizenship by descent, and IRCC's own estimate for a new application now runs close to three years. If you need proof of Canadian citizenship for a passport, a job or a family record, it pays to understand exactly which application you need and to file it correctly the first time.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What a Canadian Citizenship Certificate Is (and Is Not)</h2>

      <p>
        A citizenship certificate is the document IRCC issues to confirm that you are a Canadian citizen. Today it comes in two formats: a paper certificate mailed to you, or an electronic certificate (e-certificate) that you download from your IRCC account as a PDF. You can only hold one valid certificate at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is <strong>not</strong> a passport or travel document. IRCC says plainly that you need a valid passport or travel document to travel abroad and return to Canada. The one exception is children 15 and under crossing the Canada-U.S. border by land or sea: if they have no passport, they can show a citizenship certificate, a copy of their birth certificate or a citizenship card. For flights, IRCC's own guidance says the Canadian passport is the best document for a Canadian flying to Canada, and that people who travel on a foreign passport with only a citizenship certificate may face delays or even refusal of boarding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Older documents still count. If you have a wallet-size citizenship card, IRCC says it is still valid proof of citizenship, even though IRCC stopped issuing cards and the commemorative certificates that came with them in February 2012. An approved application for a card now produces a certificate instead.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Who Needs Proof of Canadian Citizenship?</h2>

      <p>
        Most people born in Canada never need a certificate: a provincial or territorial birth certificate is accepted proof for passports and government services. A certificate matters most if you fall into one of these groups:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Born outside Canada to a Canadian parent.</strong> You may be a citizen by descent from birth, but you have no Canadian birth certificate. A citizenship certificate is how you prove it, and you need that proof to apply for a first Canadian passport.</li>
        <li><strong>Newly recognized under Bill C-3.</strong> People in the second or later generation born abroad may have become citizens automatically on December 15, 2025. IRCC recommends applying for a certificate to confirm it.</li>
        <li><strong>Naturalized citizens who need a document.</strong> If you became a citizen through a grant and later lost track of your certificate, or never received usable proof, you need a replacement.</li>
        <li><strong>Lost, stolen, damaged or out-of-date certificates.</strong> A name change, a corrected date of birth or a gender change also requires a new certificate.</li>
        <li><strong>Unsure of your status.</strong> IRCC has a "Check if you're a Canadian citizen" tool. If your status is unclear, a certificate application is how IRCC formally decides it. If you are not a citizen, you get a refusal letter and the fee is not refunded.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Adopted children and adults born outside Canada are a special case: IRCC says not to apply directly for a certificate for them. They apply for a grant of citizenship for adoptees instead, and IRCC returns certificate applications filed for them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bill-c3" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Bill C-3 and Citizenship by Descent: What Changed</h2>

      <p>
        For years, the "first-generation limit" meant a Canadian born abroad generally could not pass citizenship to a child also born abroad. In December 2023 the Ontario Superior Court of Justice ruled that the limit was unconstitutional for many people. IRCC introduced an interim measure in March 2025, and Bill C-3 took effect on <strong>December 15, 2025</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Your situation</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Rule under Bill C-3 (per IRCC)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Already a citizen before December 15, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Still a citizen; nothing changes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Born abroad before December 15, 2025, second or later generation, to a Canadian parent</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Most people are automatically citizens; apply for a certificate to confirm</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Born abroad on or after December 15, 2025 to a Canadian parent who was also born abroad</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Citizen only if the Canadian parent spent at least 1,095 days in Canada before the birth; the guide lists form CIT 0555 for this case</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Adopted abroad on or after December 15, 2025, second or later generation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Parent needs 1,095 days in Canada before the adoption; may apply for a direct grant</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Applied under the interim measure and still waiting</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No new application needed; IRCC processes it under the new rules</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Bill C-3 is a large part of why the queue is so long. If you think you became a citizen through these changes, apply as soon as you have your family documents together, because each generation in your line has to be proven.
      </p>

      <h2 id="apply" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Apply: Online or Paper Form CIT 0001</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Who can apply online</h3>
      <p>
        IRCC's online application is open to you if you were born on or after February 15, 1977 and either became a citizen by naturalization on or after April 17, 2009, or were born outside Canada to a Canadian parent. Parents can apply online for up to three children under 18 at once, if all have the same reason for applying. You need an email address, a scanner or digital camera, and a credit card or Canadian debit card. Once you start, you have 60 days to submit, and you must not apply online if you already sent a paper application for the same request.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Who must use paper</h3>
      <p>
        You must use the paper package if, among other cases, you were naturalized on or before April 16, 2009, you are a legal guardian applying for a minor, you are replacing or updating a certificate, you are applying for four or more children, or you don't know details about your parents or grandparents. The paper form is <strong>CIT 0001</strong>, "Application for a Citizenship Certificate for Adults and Minors (Proof of Citizenship) Under Section 3," sent with the CIT 0014 document checklist. Where you mail it depends on the country you live in; the application page has a country selector, and people outside Canada and the U.S. may submit through a Canadian embassy, high commission or consulate.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Step by step</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Confirm you are likely a citizen with IRCC's citizenship check tool and the Bill C-3 rules above.</li>
        <li>Gather proof for every link in your chain: your own birth record and the birth and citizenship records of each Canadian parent, grandparent or other ancestor you rely on.</li>
        <li>Get two identical citizenship photos (paper applications) and two pieces of ID.</li>
        <li>Pay the $75 fee online and keep the receipt.</li>
        <li>Submit online, or mail the paper package with colour copies and the receipt.</li>
        <li>Wait for the acknowledgement of receipt, then track your file.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="documents" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Documents, Photos and Fee</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Item</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">What IRCC asks for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Proof of parentage and parent's citizenship</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Records issued by the original authority, such as a provincial or territorial birth certificate, for each Canadian parent, grandparent or ancestor in your claim</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Identification</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Two pieces of valid ID showing name and date of birth, one with a photo; birth certificates, SIN cards, bank cards and old citizenship certificates are not accepted as ID</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Photos</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Two identical printed citizenship photos for paper applications, not stapled or glued to the form</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Translations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">English or French translation plus the translator's affidavit for any other language; family members cannot translate</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Existing certificates</td><td class="px-4 py-3">All original citizenship or naturalization certificates you hold</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Fee</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$75 per person, paid online (as of November 14, 2026)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        If you cannot get an official record, IRCC wants a written explanation and proof that you tried. Hospital birth records, baptismal records and census records can help, but third-party records cannot support an application on their own. Send clear colour copies, not originals, unless the guide asks otherwise. The $75 fee cannot be refunded once IRCC starts processing.
      </p>

      <h2 id="processing" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Citizenship Certificate Processing Time and How to Check Your Status</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC's processing-time tool, using data last updated on October 9, 2026 (checked November 14, 2026), estimated <strong>about 34 months</strong> for a new citizenship certificate application, with <strong>about 130,700 people</strong> waiting. A search of citizenship records was listed at 18 months in data updated November 12, 2026. These are estimates for new applications, they change monthly, and an incomplete file takes longer. For how IRCC calculates these figures across all its programs, see our guide to <a href="/articles/ircc-processing-times-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">IRCC processing times in Canada</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After IRCC checks your application is complete, you get an acknowledgement of receipt (AOR). Online applicants find the AOR and their unique client identifier (UCI) in their IRCC account and track the file there. Paper applicants can check their status online once the AOR arrives. IRCC says urgent processing may be possible in some cases; the conditions are listed in its Help Centre, so a travel date alone is not a guarantee.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a decision is made, an e-certificate (or a refusal letter) appears as a link in your IRCC secure account; some paper applicants get the link by email. Paper certificates go by mail to an address in Canada or the U.S., or to the embassy, high commission or consulate where you applied if you live elsewhere.
      </p>

      <h2 id="e-certificate" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">E-Certificate or Paper Certificate?</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC began testing the electronic citizenship certificate in June 2022, and since January 2023 most applicants can choose it when they apply for citizenship, for a certificate or to resume citizenship. You select the format on the paper form or in the online application. Stateless applicants born to a Canadian parent receive paper only.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>E-certificate:</strong> a PDF you download from the IRCC Portal. IRCC says it is always valid proof of citizenship. Print it on letter-size (8.5 x 11 inch) paper, portrait, with both pages fully visible, and don't photocopy or laminate the printout.</li>
        <li><strong>Paper certificate:</strong> mailed to you. Keep it flat and unlaminated, since laminated proof may not be accepted for a passport.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Either is acceptable proof; the e-certificate simply avoids mail delays and the risk of losing the original.
      </p>

      <h2 id="replace" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Replacing a Lost Certificate or Updating Your Details</h2>

      <p>
        If your certificate is lost, stolen, damaged or destroyed, or you need to change your name, date of birth, country of birth or gender, you apply on paper with the same CIT 0001 form and the $75 fee. If IRCC approves it, your current certificate is cancelled. If you are replacing an e-certificate, IRCC requires you to delete and destroy every printed or digital copy when you submit, and not to download another copy until a decision is made. Given the current wait, keep a scanned copy of any certificate you receive and store the original safely.
      </p>

      <h2 id="passport" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Using Your Citizenship Certificate for a Canadian Passport</h2>

      <p>
        A first adult passport requires proof of Canadian citizenship (a renewal does not). If you were born outside Canada, accepted proof includes a citizenship certificate, a certificate of naturalization, a certificate of registration of birth abroad or a certificate of retention of Canadian citizenship.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>A <strong>copy</strong> is accepted if the certificate was issued on or after February 1, 2012, or its number begins with K or X.</li>
        <li>The <strong>original</strong> is required if the certificate was issued on or before January 31, 2012.</li>
        <li>An e-certificate must be printed for a new adult passport application.</li>
        <li>Originals are returned after verification: right away in person, or by mail in a separate envelope, which can take up to 6 weeks.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because the certificate step can take years, people born abroad to a Canadian parent should start it well before they need a passport.
      </p>

      <h2 id="grant" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Not a Citizen Yet? The Citizenship Grant for Permanent Residents</h2>

      <p>
        A citizenship certificate only confirms citizenship you already have. Permanent residents become citizens through a different application, the citizenship grant (naturalization). At the end of that process, after the oath, you receive your certificate. IRCC's main requirements are:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>At least 1,095 days (3 years) physically in Canada during the 5 years before you apply, with at least 730 of those days as a permanent resident. Days as a temporary resident or protected person count as half days, up to 365 days.</li>
        <li>Filed taxes for at least 3 of those 5 years, if required.</li>
        <li>For ages 18 to 54: English or French at Canadian Language Benchmark level 4 and a citizenship test.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The adult grant fee is $653 (processing plus right of citizenship fee) and $100 for a minor, as of November 14, 2026. IRCC's tool estimated about 12 months for grant applications (data of October 9, 2026). The first step, of course, is permanent residence; our guide on <a href="/articles/how-to-become-permanent-resident-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to become a permanent resident of Canada</a> covers the routes, and <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how long it takes to get PR</a> covers the timelines.
      </p>
      <p>
        Still working toward PR in British Columbia? Estimate your Skills Immigration registration score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-amber-700 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> and compare it with recent cutoffs on the <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Sources</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base">
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/proof-citizenship.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Proof of citizenship, IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/proof-citizenship/apply.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Apply for a citizenship certificate: how to apply, IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/application/application-forms-guides/guide-0001-application-citizenship-certificate-adults-minors-proof-citizenship-section-3.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Guide CIT 0001: Application for a citizenship certificate (proof of citizenship), IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/proof-citizenship/after-apply-next-steps.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Citizenship certificate: after you apply, IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/proof-citizenship/valid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Valid proof of citizenship, IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/proof-citizenship/valid/e-certificate.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Learn more about the electronic citizenship certificate, IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/canadian-citizenship/act-changes/rules-2025.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Changes to citizenship rules (Bill C-3), IRCC</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ircc.canada.ca/english/information/fees/fees.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">IRCC fee list</a></li>
